course: biology
lesson: 344
title: Honey and the beekeeper's year
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A beekeeper's year follows the bees. On the first warm, calm day of spring she lifts the lid, puffs a little smoke and looks for tiny eggs standing like grains of rice in the cells, the sign that the queen is laying. By midsummer the colony can grow from about 10,000 bees to 50,000 or more.
explain: Honey is made from nectar. Bees carry it home, pass it from bee to bee and fan it with their wings until most of the water is gone. One worker makes only about a twelfth of a teaspoon in her whole life, so a jar of honey is the work of thousands of bees.
explain: In late summer the beekeeper slices the wax caps off heavy frames and spins the honey out in a drum called an extractor. In autumn she checks for mites and weighs the hive, and in winter the bees huddle in a ball and shiver to keep warm, living on the honey they saved.
explain: Look closely at one honeybee and you can count six legs, four wings and five eyes. And the cartoon bear who eats honey straight from a pot? Real honey sits in wax cells, and the bees would not hand it over so politely.
fact: In spring a beekeeper checks that the queen is laying by looking for tiny eggs like grains of rice at the bottom of cells, and waits for a warm, calm day so the babies are not chilled; the colony grows from about 10,000 to 50,000 or more by midsummer.
fact: Smoke hides the alarm smell of guard bees, and bees gorge on honey when they smell smoke, so they are fuller and calmer.
fact: A honeybee has six legs, four wings and five eyes, two big ones on the sides and three tiny ones on top of its head, and its two pairs of wings hook together in flight.
fact: Beekeepers mark the queen with a coloured dot whose colour shows the year she was born, using white, yellow, red, green and blue in turn.
fact: A queen can live several years and lay more than a thousand eggs a day in summer, while a summer worker, always a female, lives about six weeks.
fact: An extractor is a drum that spins honey out of the uncapped cells, saving the wax comb so the bees can refill it, and a strong hive in a good summer can give about 20 kilograms or more.
fact: Bees turn nectar into honey by passing it from bee to bee, adding body chemicals and fanning it until most of the water is gone, and one worker makes only about a twelfth of a teaspoon in her life.
fact: The colour and taste of honey depend on the flowers the bees visited; heather honey from Scottish moors is thick like jelly, and manuka honey comes from a New Zealand bush.
fact: Honey that turns hard and grainy has crystallised and is not bad; it has so much sugar and so little water that germs cannot grow, and warm water melts the crystals.
fact: The varroa mite, about the size of a pinhead, feeds on bees and spreads bee diseases; it came from Asia and spread around the world.
fact: In autumn the beekeeper lifts the hive to check its weight, to be sure the bees have enough food for winter.
fact: Honeybees do not hibernate; in winter they huddle in a ball and shiver their flight muscles to make heat, and winter workers can live several months.
fact: Honey must not be given to babies under one year, because it can carry tiny resting germs that a baby's gut cannot yet stop.
exercise: What does a beekeeper look for in spring to know the queen is laying? | Tiny eggs like grains of rice at the bottom of cells | The queen's high little song, which she sings only at dawn | Honey dripping out of the hive door | Dead bees on the ground
exercise: Why does a beekeeper wait for a warm, calm day to open the hive? | A cold day would chill the babies | Bees only ever sting on warm, sunny days, never on cold ones | Honey freezes on calm days | The smoker only works in the sun
exercise: Why does smoke make bees calmer? | It hides their alarm smell and they fill up on honey | It puts the bees to sleep for ever, so they cannot move | It makes them think night has come and it is bedtime | It washes their stings away
exercise: How many legs, wings and eyes does a honeybee have? | Six legs, four wings and five eyes | Six legs, just two wings and two eyes | Six legs, two wings and only two eyes | Four legs, four wings and three eyes
exercise: How do a bee's two pairs of wings beat as one? | They hook together with a row of tiny hooks | They are glued to each other with a little honey | The bee only ever uses one pair of its wings to fly | They are joined by a thread of wax
exercise: Why does a queen bee have a coloured dot on her back? | So the beekeeper can find her and know her age | It is where she was stung | Queens are born with a dot | It shows how much honey she made
exercise: How long does a summer worker bee usually live? | About six weeks | Only about six days | About six whole years | About six months
exercise: What does an extractor do? | It spins honey out of the comb and saves the wax | It melts the whole comb down and turns it into candles | It counts the bees in the hive | It blows smoke into the hive
exercise: About how much honey can a strong hive give in a good summer? | About 20 kilograms or more | About one teaspoon, and no more than that | About 2 tonnes | About one small jar
exercise: How much honey does one worker bee make in her whole life? | About a twelfth of a teaspoon | About one full jar | About a cupful | About a kilogram
exercise: How do bees turn watery nectar into thick honey? | They pass it around and fan it until most water is gone | They boil it in the cells using the heat of their bodies | They mix it with lots of pollen and wax | They leave it out in the rain
exercise: What decides the colour and taste of honey? | The flowers the bees visited | The colour of the painted hive | The age of the beekeeper himself | How fast the extractor is spun
exercise: Runny honey has gone hard and grainy. What has happened? | It has crystallised and is still good | It has gone mouldy and must be thrown away | It has turned into wax | Bees have laid eggs in it
exercise: What is a varroa mite? | A tiny pest that feeds on bees and spreads disease | A kind of honeybee that makes thick, dark red honey | A tool for opening hives | A flower bees love
exercise: Why does a beekeeper lift the hive in autumn? | To check its weight and see if the bees have enough food for winter | To shake out the drones | To move it to a warmer country | To pour out the old honey
exercise: How do honeybees stay warm in winter? | They huddle in a ball and shiver their flight muscles | They hibernate, each one alone, deep down in the ground | They fly south to Africa for the winter | They wrap themselves in wax
exercise: Why should honey not be given to a baby under one year? | It can carry resting germs a baby's gut cannot stop | It is far too thick and sticky for babies to swallow safely | Babies are allergic to sugar | It makes babies' teeth fall out
test: In Winnie-the-Pooh, Pooh eats honey from a pot all year and the bees never miss it. What does a real beekeeper know? | Bees need stored honey to live through winter, so some must be left for them | Bees make honey only for bears, so it is fine to let the bears have all of it | Bees never eat their own honey | Bees refill a hive overnight
test: In Bee Movie, Barry is a boy bee who goes to work making honey. What is wrong? | The honey-making workers are all female | Nothing, the boy bees make all the honey | Bees do not make honey; they collect it from flowers ready-made | Honey is made by the queen bee alone in her cell
test: Count them: 3 honeybees sit on a flower. How many legs, wings and eyes are there? | 18 legs, 12 wings and 15 eyes | 18 legs, 6 wings and 6 eyes | 24 legs, 12 wings and 15 eyes | 18 legs, 12 wings and 6 eyes
test: «bees are asleep all winter like bears, yeah?» What is the best answer? | No, honeybees do not hibernate; they huddle and shiver to stay warm | Yes, they curl up and sleep in holes under the ground until spring comes | Yes, but they wake up every hour | No, they fly to hot countries
test: About how many worker bees' lifetimes of work go into one teaspoon of honey? | About 12 | About 1 | About 100 | About 1000
test: «this honey's gone all hard, bin it» Is that the right thing to do? | No, it has just crystallised; warm water melts it again | Yes, it has gone off and is now poisonous, so nobody should eat it | Yes, bees have spoiled it | No, but it is now only good for candles
test: Why can honey keep for years while fruit juice goes off in days? | Honey has so much sugar and so little water that germs cannot grow | Honey is kept in the dark | Bees add a poison to it | Juice is made in factories
test: A town is full of lime trees in flower. What kind of honey would bees there make? | Honey that tastes of lime tree flowers | Honey that tastes sharp and sour, of lemons | Green honey made from the lime tree's leaves | Exactly the same honey as anywhere else
test: «dunno why he's puffing smoke at em, just makes em angry» What does the smoke really do? | It hides the alarm smell and makes them fill up on honey, so they calm down | It makes them angrier so they fly off | It kills the guard bees at the door | It is only there to keep the beekeeper warm on cold mornings while he works
test: What would happen to many hives if beekeepers stopped caring for them against varroa mites? | Many would sicken and die | Nothing, mites only bite people | The bees would make more honey | The mites would make honey instead
test: «my auntie's about to give the baby a spoon of honey for her cough» What should you do? | Tell a grown-up straight away, honey is not for babies under one | Nothing, because honey is always safe and good for a cough at any age | Give the baby more honey | Add some sugar so it works faster
test: Why do you think winter bees live so much longer than summer bees? | They do not wear themselves out flying and foraging all day | They eat a special winter food | They are a different kind of insect | They sleep in the ground all winter
exam: Identify the principal reason for which smoke is employed by the beekeeper at the entrance of the hive. | It conceals the alarm odour of the guard bees, whereupon the bees consume honey and become calmer | It renders the bees permanently unconscious | It removes the stings of the bees | It warms the brood during inspection
exam: State the number of legs, wings and eyes possessed by an individual honeybee. | Six legs, four wings and five eyes | Six legs, two wings and two big eyes | Eight legs, four wings and five eyes | Six legs, four wings and only two eyes
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of honeybees during winter? | They do not hibernate but cluster together and generate heat by shivering the flight muscles | They hibernate individually beneath the soil until the temperature rises again in spring | They migrate to warmer regions | They perish entirely and are replaced in spring
exam: Ascertain the reason for which honey ought not to be administered to infants under one year of age. | It may contain dormant bacteria which the immature gut of an infant cannot suppress | Its sugar content is toxic to all infants | Its viscosity obstructs the infant's throat | It causes the immediate loss of infant teeth
